  {"title": "A pair-swap cipher",
   "teach": "Swap every pair of characters. Spaces get turned into stars first so the word breaks do not give the message away. The step of 2 in <code>range</code> gives 0, 2, 4 and skips the odd indexes, because each pass handles two characters.</p><p>The gate in the middle is there for messages with an odd number of characters. The loop always reaches for <code>message[i + 1]</code>, and on an odd-length message the last pass would reach one past the end. So if the length leaves a remainder of 1, one star is added to make the last pair complete. The code runs on two messages so you can see the gate stay closed for one and open for the other.",
   "code": 'for message in ["MEET AT NOON", "SEE YOU"]:\n    message = message.replace(" ", "*")\n    print(f"{message} has {len(message)} characters, remainder {len(message) % 2}")\n    if len(message) % 2 == 1:\n        message = message + "*"\n        print(f"odd length, so a star is added: {message}")\n    out = ""\n    for i in range(0, len(message), 2):\n        out = out + message[i + 1] + message[i]\n    print(out)',
   "run": "py",
   "predict": "MEET AT NOON has 12 characters and SEE YOU has 7. Which message gets a star added, and what does the first pair of the first message, ME, become?",
   "reveal": "SEE YOU gets the star, because 7 leaves a remainder of 1; MEET AT NOON does not, because 12 leaves 0. ME becomes EM, and every pair comes out in the other order. Delete the gate and run again: the first message still works, and the second crashes with an IndexError on its last pass, because <code>message[7]</code> does not exist in a seven-character message."},

